> 
> ### Adaptation Cost–Benefit Modeling for Soil Management Practices
> 
> This section documents the implementation of the soil-management adaptation analysis, which evaluates the cost-effectiveness of several agricultural practices in reducing climate-related yield losses. The analysis combines cost–benefit estimates from \citet{moran2013assessing} with spatially disaggregated agricultural production data for the UK.
> 
> #### 1. Adaptation Options and Crop–Practice Matrix
> 
> Adaptation costs and benefits for soil management practices are drawn from \citet{moran2013assessing}. That study provides estimates for the following practices (hereafter “solutions”):
> 
> - Improved drainage (“drainage”)
> - Compacted soils management (“compacted soils”)
> - Cover cropping (“cover crop”)
> - Shallow ploughing (“shallow ploughing”)
> - Spring cultivation (“spring cultivation”)
> - Contour ploughing (“contour ploughing”)
> 
> Each practice is evaluated for four crop types:
> 
> - Winter wheat (split into “free draining” and “required drainage” soils)
> - Spring barley (split into “free draining” and “required drainage” soils)
> - Potatoes
> - Carrots
> 
> Not all practices are applicable to all crop–soil combinations. The feasible set of practices by crop is encoded as:
> 
> - Winter wheat – free draining: shallow ploughing, contour ploughing
> - Winter wheat – required drainage: drainage, compacted soils, shallow ploughing, contour ploughing
> - Spring barley – free draining: shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, contour ploughing
> - Spring barley – required drainage: drainage, compacted soils, shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, contour ploughing
> - Potatoes: drainage, compacted soils, cover crop, shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, contour ploughing
> - Carrots: cover crop, shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, contour ploughing
> 
> In the code, this is captured in a `possible.solutions` table with one row per feasible (crop, solution) pair.
> 
> #### 2. Cost Data
> 
> All costs are expressed per hectare and then treated in million GBP at the national scale, as reported by \citet{moran2013assessing}. Two cost components are distinguished:
> 
> - Capital expenditure (capex): non-recurring per-hectare investment costs.
> - Operating expenditure (opex): recurring per-hectare annual implementation or maintenance costs.
> 
> Capex and opex ranges (low and high values per hectare) are specified for each solution:
> 
> - Drainage: both capex and opex are non-zero.
> - Compacted soils: opex only.
> - Cover crop: opex only.
> - Shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, contour ploughing: zero opex (marginal implementation costs treated as zero in \citet{moran2013assessing}).
> 
> Formally, for solution \(s\), the per-hectare capex and opex ranges are:
> 
> \[
> \text{Capex}_{s}^{\text{lo}},\ \text{Capex}_{s}^{\text{hi}}, \qquad 
> \text{Opex}_{s}^{\text{lo}},\ \text{Opex}_{s}^{\text{hi}}.
> \]
> 
> \citet{moran2013assessing} also reports, for each solution–crop pair \((s,c)\), a total implementation cost and associated benefits under pessimistic and optimistic price scenarios, all in million GBP. These are read from a cleaned version of the original SRUC table:
> 
> - \(C_{s,c}\): implementation cost (million GBP)
> - \(B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}}\): crop benefit under pessimistic price scenario (million GBP)
> - \(B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}\): crop benefit under optimistic price scenario (million GBP)
> 
> The code checks that pessimistic and optimistic costs are identical for each \((s,c)\) pair and stores these in a combined table `df2`.
> 
> To avoid problems with exact zero-cost solutions and to ensure that all solutions are visible in the marginal cost curve, we add a constant offset of 5 million GBP to the reported costs:
> 
> \[
> C'_{s,c} = C_{s,c} + 5\ \text{million GBP}.
> \]
> 
> \(C'_{s,c}\) is then used as the effective implementation cost in subsequent calculations.
> 
> #### 3. Regional Agricultural Production Data
> 
> To normalize benefits by the economic value at risk, we construct regional production values for cereals, potatoes, and total crops, and then further allocate these to the specific crop categories used in the adaptation analysis.
> 
> ##### 3.1 NUTS2-level production values
> 
> We use regional agricultural accounts from an official spreadsheet (`agr_r_accts__custom_2148028_spreadsheet.xlsx`) with three relevant sheets:
> 
> - Sheet 1: cereal production (million GBP),
> - Sheet 5: potato production (million GBP),
> - Sheet 10: total crop production (million GBP).
> 
> For each NUTS2 region \(r\), the production values are averaged over 2010–2012:
> 
> \[
> \begin{aligned}
> P^{\text{cereal}}_r &= \frac{1}{3}\sum_{y=2010}^{2012} P^{\text{cereal}}_{r,y}, \\
> P^{\text{potato}}_r &= \frac{1}{3}\sum_{y=2010}^{2012} P^{\text{potato}}_{r,y}, \\
> P^{\text{croptl}}_r &= \frac{1}{3}\sum_{y=2010}^{2012} P^{\text{croptl}}_{r,y}.
> \end{aligned}
> \]
> 
> These are assembled into a single NUTS2-level table with variables
> \(P^{\text{cereal}}_r\), \(P^{\text{potato}}_r\), and \(P^{\text{croptl}}_r\).
> 
> ##### 3.2 Disaggregation to county (GADM3) level
> 
> We disaggregate NUTS2-level values to GADM level-3 units (“counties”) using an area-based weighting scheme:
> 
> 1. A crosswalk (`gadm2nuts.csv`) associates each GADM3 polygon \(i\) with a NUTS2 region \(r(i)\).
> 2. Polygon areas \(A_i\) are taken from a prepared GADM3 shapefile (`gadm36_GBR_3-withattr.shp`).
> 3. For each NUTS2 region \(r\), its total NUTS2 production values are allocated to counties proportionally to area:
> 
> \[
> \begin{aligned}
> P^{\text{croptl}}_i &= P^{\text{croptl}}_{r(i)} \cdot \frac{A_i}{\sum_{j: r(j)=r(i)} A_j}, \\
> P^{\text{cereal}}_i &= P^{\text{cereal}}_{r(i)} \cdot \frac{A_i}{\sum_{j: r(j)=r(i)} A_j}, \\
> P^{\text{potato}}_i &= P^{\text{potato}}_{r(i)} \cdot \frac{A_i}{\sum_{j: r(j)=r(i)} A_j}.
> \end{aligned}
> \]
> 
> This yields county-level production values \(P^{\text{croptl}}_i\), \(P^{\text{cereal}}_i\), and \(P^{\text{potato}}_i\).
> 
> ##### 3.3 Allocation between wheat and barley, and construction of crop categories
> 
> We then allocate county-level cereal production to winter wheat and spring barley under free-draining and required-drainage soil conditions.
> 
> First, NUTS1-level production tonnages for wheat and spring barley are obtained from `uk-cops-webseries-241212.xlsx`, for the years 2010–2012. For each NUTS1 region \(R\):
> 
> \[
> \begin{aligned}
> T^{\text{wheat}}_R &= \frac{1}{3}\sum_{y=2010}^{2012} T^{\text{wheat}}_{R,y}, \\
> T^{\text{barley}}_R &= \frac{1}{3}\sum_{y=2010}^{2012} T^{\text{barley}}_{R,y}.
> \end{aligned}
> \]
> 
> Each county \(i\) is mapped to a NUTS1 region \(R(i)\) via the first three characters of its NUTS2 code. Following \citet{anthony2012}, we assume that 41% of arable land is free-draining. Using this fraction, the county-level cereal production \(P^{\text{cereal}}_i\) is split between wheat and barley, and between free-draining and required-drainage soils as:
> 
> \[
> \begin{aligned}
> P^{\text{W Wheat – free draining}}_i 
> &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.41 \times \frac{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}, \\
> P^{\text{W Wheat – required drainage}}_i 
> &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.59 \times \frac{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}, \\
> P^{\text{S Barley – free draining}}_i 
> &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.41 \times \frac{T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}, \\
> P^{\text{S Barley – required drainage}}_i 
> &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.59 \times \frac{T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}.
> \end{aligned}
> \]
> 
> For potatoes, we directly use the potato production value:
> 
> \[
> P^{\text{Potatoes}}_i = P^{\text{potato}}_i.
> \]
> 
> For carrots, which are not reported separately in the agricultural accounts, we conservatively assign the residual crop value after subtracting cereals and potatoes:
> 
> \[
> P^{\text{Carrots}}_i = P^{\text{croptl}}_i - P^{\text{potato}}_i - P^{\text{cereal}}_i.
> \]
> 
> This construction is intentionally conservative: any unaccounted crop value is attributed to carrots, and adaptation benefits are only applied to this residual.
> 
> Finally, for each crop category \(c\) (winter wheat – free draining, winter wheat – required drainage, spring barley – free draining, spring barley – required drainage, potatoes, carrots), we compute the total national production value:
> 
> \[
> \sum_i P^{c}_i,
> \]
> 
> which is used to normalize adaptation benefits.
> 
> #### 4. Marginal Effectiveness of Adaptation
> 
> For each feasible solution–crop pair \((s,c)\) in `possible.solutions`, marginal effectiveness is defined as avoided losses (as a percentage of total crop value) per million GBP of implementation cost.
> 
> Let:
> 
> - \(C_{s,c}\) be the implementation cost (million GBP),
> - \(B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}}\) and \(B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}\) be pessimistic and optimistic crop benefits (million GBP),
> - \(\sum_i P^{c}_i\) be the total value of crop \(c\) (million GBP).
> 
> We first define normalized avoided losses (as percentages of total crop value) under each price scenario:
> 
> \[
> L_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}} = \frac{B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}}}{\sum_i P^{c}_i}, \qquad
> L_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}} = \frac{B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}}{\sum_i P^{c}_i}.
> \]
> 
> Using the adjusted cost \(C'_{s,c} = C_{s,c} + 5\), the marginal effectiveness under pessimistic and optimistic scenarios is:
> 
> \[
