> ### Soil‑Management Adaptation: Calibration and Application
> 
> This section describes how cost–benefit information for soil management practices is calibrated to UK agricultural production data and then applied to reduce modeled climate impacts on crop yields.
> 
> #### 1. Adaptation options and crop coverage
> 
> Soil-management options are taken from \citet{moran2013assessing}: improved drainage, compacted soils management, cover crops, shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, and contour ploughing. These are evaluated for:
> 
> - winter wheat (split into free-draining and required-drainage soils),
> - spring barley (free-draining and required-drainage),
> - potatoes, and
> - carrots.
> 
> A crop–practice feasibility matrix (`possible.solutions`) specifies which practices apply to each crop–soil combination; infeasible pairs are excluded from the analysis.
> 
> #### 2. Cost and benefit calibration
> 
> From \citet{moran2013assessing}, for each solution–crop pair \((s,c)\) we extract:
> 
> - implementation cost in a given price scenario \(k \in \{\mathrm{pes},\mathrm{opt}\}\):
>   \[
>   C_{s,c}^{k} \ \text{(million GBP)},
>   \]
> - total crop benefit:
>   \[
>   B_{s,c}^{k} \ \text{(million GBP)},
>   \]
> - net present value:
>   \[
>   \text{NPV}_{s,c}^{k} \ \text{(million GBP)}.
>   \]
> 
> The cleaned table (`df2`) contains pessimistic (“pes”) and optimistic (“opt”) values, with a consistency check that \(C_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}} = C_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}\).
> 
> We use scenario averages and apply a small cost correction:
> 
> \[
> \begin{aligned}
> \overline{B}_{s,c} &= \frac{B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}} + B_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}}{2},\\[3pt]
> C'_{s,c} &= \frac{C_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}} + C_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}}{2} + 5,\\[3pt]
> \text{NPV}'_{s,c} &= \max\!\left(0, \frac{\text{NPV}_{s,c}^{\mathrm{pes}} + \text{NPV}_{s,c}^{\mathrm{opt}}}{2} - 5\right).
> \end{aligned}
> \]
> 
> The 5 million GBP offset in \(C'_{s,c}\) and \(\text{NPV}'_{s,c}\) (the same adjustment used for the MAC curve) (i) ensures zero‑cost options are visible in marginal analyses, and (ii) slightly penalizes options with very small positive NPVs.
> 
> #### 3. Spatial crop-production data
> 
> We construct spatially disaggregated crop values to normalize adaptation benefits.
> 
> 1. **NUTS2 agricultural accounts.** From official regional accounts (2010–2012 averages), we obtain for each NUTS2 region \(r\):
> 
>    \[
>    P^{\text{cereal}}_r,\quad P^{\text{potato}}_r,\quad P^{\text{croptl}}_r
>    \]
>    (million GBP for cereals, potatoes, and total crops).
> 
> 2. **Downscaling to counties.** Using a NUTS2–GADM3 crosswalk and GADM3 polygon areas \(A_i\), we disaggregate NUTS2 values to counties \(i\) by area-weighting:
>    \[
>    P^{x}_i = P^{x}_{r(i)} \cdot \frac{A_i}{\sum_{j: r(j)=r(i)} A_j}, \qquad x \in \{\text{cereal}, \text{potato}, \text{croptl}\}.
>    \]
> 
> 3. **Split cereals into wheat and barley, free-draining and required-drainage.** For each NUTS1 region \(R\), we compute mean wheat and spring barley tonnages \(T^{\text{wheat}}_R\), \(T^{\text{barley}}_R\) over 2010–2012. Each county \(i\) is linked to a NUTS1 region \(R(i)\). Following \citet{anthony2012}, 41% of arable land is assumed free‑draining:
> 
>    \[
>    \begin{aligned}
>    P^{\text{W Wheat – free}}_i 
>    &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.41 \times \frac{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}},\\
>    P^{\text{W Wheat – req}}_i 
>    &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.59 \times \frac{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}},\\
>    P^{\text{S Barley – free}}_i 
>    &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.41 \times \frac{T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}},\\
>    P^{\text{S Barley – req}}_i 
>    &= P^{\text{cereal}}_i \times 0.59 \times \frac{T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}{T^{\text{wheat}}_{R(i)} + T^{\text{barley}}_{R(i)}}.
>    \end{aligned}
>    \]
> 
> 4. **Potatoes and carrots.** Potato value is taken directly, and carrots are treated conservatively as a residual:
> 
>    \[
>    P^{\text{Potatoes}}_i = P^{\text{potato}}_i, \qquad
>    P^{\text{Carrots}}_i = P^{\text{croptl}}_i - P^{\text{potato}}_i - P^{\text{cereal}}_i.
>    \]
> 
> This yields, for each county \(i\), a value \(P^c_i\) for each crop category \(c\). The total national value of crop \(c\) is
> \[
> V^c = \sum_i P^c_i.
> \]
> 
> The resulting county–crop table is stored as `agprod5`.
> 
> #### 4. Construction of adaptation options
> 
> The function `load.agric.adapt()` combines the calibrated cost–benefit data with the spatial production data and climate impacts:
> 
> 1. Load `agprod5` and `df2` (calibration) and the impact dataset `impacts` (county-level climate impact projections by scenario, year, and measure).
> 2. Compute scenario-averaged cost, benefit, and NPV (with adjustments as in Section 2):
>    \[
>    \overline{B}_{s,c} \equiv \texttt{Crop.benefit...million.},\quad
>    C'_{s,c} \equiv \texttt{Cost...million.},\quad
>    \text{NPV}'_{s,c} \equiv \texttt{NPV...million.}.
>    \]
> 3. Reshape `agprod5` to long format so that each record is a county–crop pair \((i,c)\) with crop value
>    \(\texttt{value} = P^c_i\).
> 4. Join with `df2` on crop \(c\); this yields `options`, a table over county \(i\), crop \(c\), and solution \(s\) with:
>    - local crop value \(P^c_i\),
>    - calibrated \(\overline{B}_{s,c}\), \(C'_{s,c}\), \(\text{NPV}'_{s,c}\).
> 
> `load.agric.adapt()` returns a list \((\texttt{options}, \texttt{impacts})\).
> 
> #### 5. Budget allocation and impact reduction
> 
> Given an annual adaptation budget \(S\) (million GBP yr\(^{-1}\)), the function `agric.adapt.prop(spend.MGBPyr = S, loaded)` allocates this budget across options and translates it into reductions in modeled impacts.
> 
> ##### 5.1 Budget allocation across county–crop–solution options
> 
> For each option \((i,c,s)\) in `options`, with local crop value \(P^c_i\) and adjusted NPV \(\text{NPV}'_{s,c}\), we allocate spending proportional to \(P^c_i \times \text{NPV}'_{s,c}\):
> 
> \[
> \text{spend}_{i,c,s}
> = S \times 
> \frac{P^c_i \, \text{NPV}'_{s,c}}
>      {\sum_{i',c',s'} P^{c'}_{i'} \, \text{NPV}'_{s',c'}}.
> \]
> 
> This favors solutions that (i) apply to higher‑value crops/locations and (ii) have higher net present values.
> 
> The realized benefit of that spend is taken to scale linearly with cost up to the calibrated level:
> 
> \[
> \text{benefit}_{i,c,s}
> = \text{spend}_{i,c,s} \times \frac{\overline{B}_{s,c}}{C'_{s,c}}.
> \]
> 
> Benefits and spending are then summed over solutions at the county–crop level:
> 
> \[
> \text{benefit}_{i,c} = \sum_s \text{benefit}_{i,c,s}, \qquad
> \text{spend}_{i,c}   = \sum_s \text{spend}_{i,c,s}.
> \]
> 
> ##### 5.2 Mapping benefits to modeled climate impacts
> 
> The `impacts` table contains, for each county \(i\), scenario \(q\), year \(t\), and impact measure \(m\):
> 
> - central impact estimate \(\mu_{qtm i}\),
> - lower and upper 90% confidence bounds \(\text{ci05}_{qtm i}\), \(\text{ci95}_{qtm i}\).
> 
> These represent baseline (no‑adaptation) damages. The adaptation model proceeds in three steps.
> 
> 1. **Value-weighting impacts.** Within each \((q,t,m,i)\) group, impacts are reweighted by crop value so that counties with larger agricultural value contribute more to aggregate impacts. Operationally, for each county \(i\):
> 
>    \[
>    \mu_{qtm i}^{\text{val}} = \mu_{qtm i} \times \frac{\texttt{value}_{i}}{\sum_{j \in i} \texttt{value}_j},
>    \]
>    and similarly for \(\text{ci05}\) and \(\text{ci95}\), where the inner sum is over crop categories within the county.
> 
> 2. **Fraction of impact mitigated.** For each \((q,t,m,i)\)
