> ### Soil‑management adaptation: calibration and application
> 
> Adaptation options for soil management are taken from \citet{moran2013assessing}: improved drainage, compacted soils management, cover crops, shallow ploughing, spring cultivation, and contour ploughing. These are evaluated for winter wheat and spring barley (each split into free‑draining and required‑drainage soils), potatoes, and carrots. A feasibility matrix defines which practices are applicable to each crop–soil combination.
> 
> #### Calibration of costs, benefits, and spatial exposure
> 
> For each applicable solution–crop pair, the source provides pessimistic and optimistic estimates of implementation cost, total crop benefit, and net present value (all in million GBP). Pessimistic and optimistic costs are identical and are averaged together; pessimistic and optimistic benefits and NPVs are likewise averaged. A constant term of 5 million GBP is added to the cost and subtracted from the NPV, and the NPV is truncated below at zero. Denoting pessimistic and optimistic costs by \(C^{\mathrm{pes}}_{s,c}\) and \(C^{\mathrm{opt}}_{s,c}\), benefits by \(B^{\mathrm{pes}}_{s,c}\) and \(B^{\mathrm{opt}}_{s,c}\), and NPVs by \(\mathrm{NPV}^{\mathrm{pes}}_{s,c}\) and \(\mathrm{NPV}^{\mathrm{opt}}_{s,c}\), the calibrated quantities are
> \[
> \overline{B}_{s,c} = \frac{B^{\mathrm{pes}}_{s,c} + B^{\mathrm{opt}}_{s,c}}{2},\quad
> C'_{s,c} = \frac{C^{\mathrm{pes}}_{s,c} + C^{\mathrm{opt}}_{s,c}}{2} + 5,\quad
> \mathrm{NPV}'_{s,c} = \max\!\left(0,\frac{\mathrm{NPV}^{\mathrm{pes}}_{s,c} + \mathrm{NPV}^{\mathrm{opt}}_{s,c}}{2} - 5\right).
> \]
> 
> To normalize these against exposure, agricultural production values are constructed for each crop and county. National and regional accounts are first used to obtain 2010–2012 average values of cereals, potatoes, and total crops at NUTS2 level. These are downscaled to GADM level‑3 “county” polygons using area weights. Cereal production in each county is then split into winter wheat and spring barley, and into free‑draining and required‑drainage soils, using NUTS1‑level wheat and barley tonnages and the assumption from \citet{anthony2012} that 41% of arable land is free‑draining. Potato value is taken directly, and carrot value is defined conservatively as the residual of total crops after subtracting cereals and potatoes. This yields, for each county \(i\) and crop category \(c\), a crop value \(P^c_i\); the corresponding national value is
> \[
> V^c = \sum_i P^c_i.
> \]
> 
> #### Allocation of adaptation spending
> 
> Adaptation spending is assumed to be distributed across county–crop–solution combinations in proportion to both local crop value and the calibrated net present value of the solution. For a given annual budget \(S\) (million GBP yr\(^{-1}\)), the share assigned to solution \(s\) on crop \(c\) in county \(i\) is proportional to \(P^c_i \,\mathrm{NPV}'_{s,c}\). The implied annual spend is
> \[
> \text{spend}_{i,c,s}
> = S \times 
> \frac{P^c_i \,\mathrm{NPV}'_{s,c}}
>      {\sum_{i',c',s'} P^{c'}_{i'} \,\mathrm{NPV}'_{s',c'}}.
> \]
> 
> Realised adaptation benefits are assumed to scale linearly with spending up to the calibrated cost. Defining the benefit–cost ratio \(\overline{B}_{s,c}/C'_{s,c}\), the benefit associated with this spending is
> \[
> \text{benefit}_{i,c,s}
> = \text{spend}_{i,c,s} \times \frac{\overline{B}_{s,c}}{C'_{s,c}}.
> \]
> Benefits and spending are then aggregated over solutions at the county–crop level,
> \[
> \text{benefit}_{i,c} = \sum_s \text{benefit}_{i,c,s},\qquad
> \text{spend}_{i,c}   = \sum_s \text{spend}_{i,c,s}.
> \]
> 
> #### Translation into reduced climate impacts
> 
> Baseline climate impacts on agriculture are represented as county‑level time series of damage estimates by scenario and impact measure, including a central estimate \(\mu\) and 5th and 95th percentile bounds (\(\mathrm{ci05}\), \(\mathrm{ci95}\)). These damages are first allocated across crops within each county in proportion to the corresponding crop values \(P^c_i\), so that higher‑value crops bear a larger share of local damages.
> 
> Adaptation benefits are then expressed as a fraction of these damages. For each county, the ratio of adaptation benefit to baseline damage is computed for the central estimate and each bound; ratios are capped above at 1 (no more than full elimination of damage), and any negative ratios are set to 1 (no worsening beyond baseline). Denoting the relevant baseline damage for a given quantile by \(D\) and the corresponding aggregate benefit by \(B\), the damage fraction remaining is
> \[
> f = 1 - \min\!\left(1,\max\!\left(0,\frac{B}{D}\right)\right).
> \]
> This is calculated separately for the central estimate and the lower and upper bounds. The resulting county‑level impact factors, lying between 0 and 1, are then applied to the corresponding baseline damages to obtain post‑adaptation impacts.
> 
