> \subsubsection*{Variability and extreme events}
> 
> To represent interannual variability and the potential for extreme agricultural losses, we use annual World Bank estimates of total agricultural production in the UK (constant prices). In log terms, these values follow an approximately linear trend over 1960–2024, which we estimate via
> \[
> \log P_t = \alpha + \beta t + \varepsilon_t,
> \]
> where \(P_t\) is total UK agricultural production in year \(t\), and \(\varepsilon_t\) are residuals around the trend.
> 
> UK-wide variability is modeled as a random draw of \(\varepsilon_m\) from the empirical residuals in each year \(t\) and each Monte Carlo realization \(m\). County-level deviations are then taken to be distributed around that UK-wide deviation with the same dispersion, by drawing an additional independent residual \(\varepsilon_{im}\) from the same empirical distribution for each county \(i\). Thus, the realized damage for Monte Carlo \(m\), county \(i\), and year \(t\) is
> \[
> D_{mit} = D_{mi} \exp(\varepsilon_m + \varepsilon_{im}),
> \]
> where \(D_{mi}\) is the expected (trend) annual damage for that county and Monte Carlo realization, \(\varepsilon_m\) is shared by all counties in that year, and \(\varepsilon_{im}\) captures county-specific deviations.
> 
> UK-wide expected annual damages (EAD) are obtained by aggregating county-level damages across all Monte Carlo runs. To approximate “1-in-200 year” extremes under a high-risk climate pathway, we consider decadal windows (e.g.\ 2030–2039, 2040–2049, 2050–2059), apply the same variability structure, and for each Monte Carlo realization retain the single year with the highest UK-wide loss. These extreme-loss outcomes are summarized at both the national and county level.
> 
> Figure~\ref{fig:dataset_uk} shows UK-wide projected agricultural losses under the “Most Likely” and “High Risk” climate pathways. For each scenario, the solid lines give the evolution of EAD, with shaded bands indicating the 5th–95th percentile range. Superimposed red error bars show the corresponding 5th–95th percentile range of the decadal “1-in-200 year” extreme-loss events for selected years. Figure~\ref{fig:dataset_adm3} shows the same information disaggregated to counties: thin lines show county-level EAD trajectories, and red points indicate the upper 95th percentile of the extreme-loss distribution for each county and decade under each scenario.
> 
